---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';

/**
 * Every article byline links here. Before this page carried an author section it
 * answered nothing a reader following that byline actually wanted to know, which
 * is the weakest possible signal on YMYL tax and residence content.
 */
const personSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Person',
  name: SITE.editorial,
  jobTitle: SITE.authorRole,
  url: `${SITE.url}/about/`,
  email: SITE.email,
  knowsAbout: [
    'Greek Golden Visa',
    'Greek property law',
    'Greek property taxation',
    'Athens real estate',
    'Residence by investment',
  ],
  worksFor: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: SITE.url },
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="About Greek Invest"
  description="Who writes Greek Invest, how the research desk works, what we cover, and the limits of what an editorial site can tell you about Greek property."
  schemas={[personSchema]}
>
  <section class="site-container py-12 md:py-16 max-w-3xl">
    <h1 class="mb-6">About {SITE.name}</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        {SITE.name} publishes research-first guides for foreign buyers evaluating Athens, the Athens
        Riviera, Crete, the Cyclades and mainland Greece. We are <strong>not</strong> a developer, not
        a listing portal, and not a substitute for licensed legal or tax advice. Nothing here is
        inventory we are paid to move.
      </p>

      <h2>Who writes this site</h2>
      <p>
        Articles carry a named byline: {SITE.editorial}, {SITE.authorRole}. That is deliberate. Greek
        residence and tax rules move fast enough that unsigned content ages badly and nobody is
        accountable for it. If a figure on this site is wrong, there is a person to write to, and the
        address is <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The desk is editorial, not advisory. We read the primary Greek sources, check the numbers, and
        write them up in English for buyers who do not read the Government Gazette. The regulated work
        that follows — title searches, the engineer's certificate, the notarial deed, the Ministry
        file — is done by licensed Greek lawyers, notaries and civil engineers, not by us.
      </p>

      <h2>What we cover</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Golden Visa tiers under Law 5100/2024, the 120 m² main-usable-area rule, and where a parcel actually falls</li>
        <li>Transfer taxes, ENFIA, the 2026 rental income scale, and the Article 5A and 5B special tax regimes</li>
        <li>Market comparisons: Athens against the islands, off-plan against resale, long lets against short-term rental</li>
        <li>Neutral reviews of individual developments, written from public developer material and independent checks</li>
        <li>Two maintained datasets: Golden Visa issuance statistics and a regional price index</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How a page gets published</h2>
      <p>
        Every guide is written against primary sources, dated, and put through four checks before it
        goes live: the correct Law 5100/2024 tier for the cadastral parcel, the 120 m² of κύρια χρήση
        on the engineer's certificate, a single title deed, and a traceable transfer of funds under
        Circular 1/2026. The <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a> sets out each one in full,
        along with the sources we prioritise and how figures are dated.
      </p>

      <h2>What we don't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Push specific developer inventory, or take a referral fee without disclosing it</li>
        <li>Guarantee yields, capital appreciation, or visa outcomes</li>
        <li>Provide personalised financial, legal, or tax advice</li>
        <li>Publish a figure we cannot trace to a source we can name</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we make money</h2>
      <p>
        When you ask for a shortlist or a consultation, your enquiry may be passed to licensed Greek
        lawyers and partner agents who can do the regulated work. That referral is how the site is
        funded, and it does not buy anyone editorial placement. The
        <a href="/privacy-policy/">privacy policy</a> sets out exactly what is shared and when.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Greek thresholds and tax bands change, sometimes twice in a year. If you find a figure that has
        moved, or one that was wrong to begin with, email <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>
        and we will correct the page and update its date. Or start with the
        <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
